     rriippdd..ccoonnff — RIP routing daemon configuration file

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ripd(8) daemon implements the Routing Information Protocol version 2
     as described in RFC 2453.

     The rriippdd..ccoonnff config file is divided into the following main sections:

     _M_a_c_r_o_s
           Definitions of variables that can be used later, simplifying the
           configuration file.

     _G_l_o_b_a_l _c_o_n_f_i_g_u_r_a_t_i_o_n
           Global settings for ripd(8).

     _I_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e_s _c_o_n_f_i_g_u_r_a_t_i_o_n
           Interface-specific parameters.

   MMaaccrrooss
     A macro is defined with a command of the form _n_a_m_e=_v_a_l_u_e.  The macro _n_a_m_e
     can contain letters, digits, and underscores and cannot be a reserved
     word (for example, ccoosstt).  Within unquoted arguments, the string $_n_a_m_e is
     later expanded to _v_a_l_u_e.

     For example:

           hi="2"
           interface em0 {
                   cost $hi
           }

   GGlloobbaall ccoonnffiigguurraattiioonn
     Global settings concerns the main behaviour of the daemon.

     ffiibb--pprriioorriittyy _p_r_i_o
             Set the routing priority to _p_r_i_o.  The default is 40.

     ffiibb--uuppddaattee (yyeess|nnoo)
             If set to nnoo, do not update the Forwarding Information Base,
             a.k.a. the kernel routing table.  The default is yyeess.

     rrddoommaaiinn _t_a_b_l_e_i_d
             Specifies the routing table ripd(8) should modify.  Table 0 is
             the default table.

     [nnoo] rreeddiissttrriibbuuttee (ssttaattiicc|ccoonnnneecctteedd|ddeeffaauulltt)
     [nnoo] rreeddiissttrriibbuuttee _p_r_e_f_i_x
     [nnoo] rreeddiissttrriibbuuttee rrttllaabbeell _l_a_b_e_l
             If set to ccoonnnneecctteedd, routes to directly attached networks will be
             announced over RIP.  If set to ssttaattiicc, static routes will be an‐
             nounced over RIP.  If set to ddeeffaauulltt, a default route pointing to
             this router will be announced over RIP.  It is possible to spec‐
             ify a network range with _p_r_e_f_i_x; networks need to be part of that
             range to be redistributed.  Additionally it is possible to redis‐
             tribute based on route labels using the rrttllaabbeell keyword.  By de‐
             fault no additional routes will be announced over RIP.

             rreeddiissttrriibbuuttee statements are evaluated in sequential order, from
             first to last.  The first matching rule decides if a route should
             be redistributed or not.  Matching rules starting with nnoo will
             force the route to be not announced.

     sspplliitt--hhoorriizzoonn (ssiimmppllee|ppooiissoonneedd|nnoonnee)
             If set to ssiimmppllee, do not redistribute routes to the interface
             from which they were learned.  If set to ppooiissoonneedd, redistribute
             routes to the interface from which they were learned, by forcing
             the metric to infinity.  Both these behaviours can resolve rout‐
             ing loops when a router goes down.  The default is ppooiissoonneedd.

     ttrriiggggeerreedd--uuppddaatteess (yyeess|nnoo)
             Immediately trigger responses when a route changes.  The default
             is nnoo.

   IInntteerrffaacceess ccoonnffiigguurraattiioonn
     Each interface can have several parameters configured individually, oth‐
     erwise they are inherited.

           interface em0 {
                   cost 9
                   auth-type none
           }
           interface ix1

     Interface-specific parameters are listed below.

     aauutthh--kkeeyy _k_e_y
             Set the authentication key for ssiimmppllee authentication.  Up to 16
             characters can be specified.

     aauutthh--mmdd _k_e_y_-_i_d _k_e_y
             Set the authentication _k_e_y_-_i_d and _k_e_y for ccrryypptt authentication.
             The valid range for _k_e_y_-_i_d is 0-255.  Up to 16 characters can be
             specified for _k_e_y.  Multiple keys may be specified.

     aauutthh--mmdd--kkeeyyiidd _k_e_y_-_i_d
             Configure the _k_e_y_-_i_d to use for ccrryypptt authentication.  The valid
             range for _k_e_y_-_i_d is 0-255.  The default key-id is 1.

     aauutthh--ttyyppee (nnoonnee|ssiimmppllee|ccrryypptt)
             Set the authentication type.  The default is nnoonnee.  Simple au‐
             thentication uses a plaintext password, up to 16 characters.
             Crypt authentication uses an MD5 hash.

     ccoosstt _n_u_m_b_e_r
             Cost of the network connected through this interface.  Values
             must be between 1 and 16, inclusive.

     ddeemmoottee _g_r_o_u_p
             Increase the carp(4) demotion counter by 1 on the given interface
             group, usually _c_a_r_p, when the interface state is going down.  The
             demotion counter will be decreased when the interface state is
             active again.

     ppaassssiivvee
             Prevent transmission and reception of RIP packets on this inter‐
             face.

